## Method and reasoning
Sum the first k values and initialize the answer from that window, including if it is negative. When advancing, add the entering value and subtract the leaving one. Each move then takes constant time.

## Walk through the sample
The three length-3 sums are −14, −11 and −12. The maximum is −11. Starting the best sum at zero would silently allow a window that was never present.

## Complexity
Total time O(n), with O(n) storage for the array.

## Reference implementation
~~~
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    int n, k;
    cin >> n >> k;
    vector<long long> a(n);
    for (auto &x : a)
        cin >> x;
    long long sum = accumulate(a.begin(), a.begin() + k, 0LL), best = sum;
    for (int r = k; r < n; ++r) {
        sum += a[r] - a[r - k];
        best = max(best, sum);
    }
    cout << best << '\n';
}
~~~
